From 4c5180cd89cda7a212bba31eac3b60dfa3d6b3b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 06:09:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expand: a proc macro's input has commas only between list
 entries

tor-ptmgr 0.47.0 (arti 2.7.0) config.rs:23 `#[derive_deftly(TorConfig)]`
with `#[deftly(tor_config(default, setter(strip_option)))] path:
Option<CfgPath>`: `MACRO<..::strip_option> error:0: compile_error!
"'strip_option' only works on a type that is an Option<X>"`.

The visitor that hands an item to a proc macro wrote a comma after every
entry of every list it printed: generic arguments (`Option < CfgPath , >`),
tuple and fn-pointer types, `for<'a,>`, struct, enum and union bodies,
fn arguments, use lists, slice/tuple/struct patterns and where clauses.
syn takes a trailing comma everywhere, so nothing had noticed; but
derive-deftly re-emits a field's type (`$ftype`) into tor-config's
`strip_option!{..}`, whose `Option < $t:ty >` arm cannot match the comma
and whose `$t:ty` error arm can. rustc hands over the tokens as written,
and a trailing comma is only ever written by choice.

A comma now only separates entries, and is kept where it is syntax:
a one-element tuple type `(T,)` and tuple pattern `(p,)`. The fn-pointer
type and the higher-ranked bounds go through the AST printers (re-lexed
as proc-macro input), fixed the same way.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
---
 
diff --git a/src/ast/ast.cpp b/src/ast/ast.cpp
index ad51d6fd..c653cee8 100644
--- a/src/ast/ast.cpp
+++ b/src/ast/ast.cpp
@@ -607,7 +607,11 @@ Item Item::clone() const
     {
         os << "for<";
         for(const auto& l : x.m_lifetimes)
-            os << l << ",";
+        {
+            if( &l != x.m_lifetimes.data() )
+                os << ",";
+            os << l;
+        }
         os << "> ";
     }
     return os;
